verify your corporation has been restored to good status.

.why = confirm Delaware processed the revival before you rely on the corporation for contracts, bank accounts, or operations.

---

## check status

search: https://icis.corp.delaware.gov/ecorp/entitysearch/namesearch.aspx

enter your corporation name or file number.

---

## what to look for

| field | void | restored |
|-------|------|----------|
| status | "Void, AR's or Tax Delinquent" | "Good Standing" |
| tax due | $XXX,XXX | $0 or current year only |

---

## if still void

| days since submission | action |
|-----------------------|--------|
| < 5 business days | wait (standard) |
| 5-10 business days | call to check status |
| > 10 business days | escalate |

contact: (302) 739-3073

### 🚨 but "wait (standard)" is the WRONG default if the instrument was suspended

⛔ **the < 5 business days row and § 103(c)(4)'s cure window are the same five
days**, and they point opposite ways:

| if the instrument is… | those five days are |
|---|---|
| in the queue, normal | 🕐 a wait. do naught |
| **held in suspension** | ⏳ **the entire cure window.** spend it and the original filing date is gone |

⭐ **so the two states are indistinguishable from the outside, and the cheap
action is only correct in one of them.** the tie-breaker is a notice you may not
have received — under § 103(c)(4) it goes to *the filer*, who may be an agent.

🎯 **the rule this yields: if you filed through an agent, or if you cannot
confirm the instrument is in the normal queue, CALL on day one or two — not on
day five.** a call that confirms "in the queue" costs a few minutes. a call that
arrives on day six, after a suspension you were never told about, costs the
filing date and every void-window day behind it.

⚠️ this is the one place in the route where the conservative move is to act
*early* rather than wait. everywhere else, patience is free.

---

## obtain Certificate of Good Status (optional)

if you need proof for banks, contracts, or investors:

### ⛔ FIRST — § 502(f) bars the certificate on TWO independent grounds

> this stone priced the certificate and never stated what
> **prevents** its issue. the bar is statutory, and one half of it is not about
> money at all.

> "The Secretary of State **shall not issue certificates of good standing** that
> pertain to any corporation that **has an unpaid franchise tax balance** due to
> the State **or does not have on file a completed annual franchise tax report
> for the relevant time period**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 502(f), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

read the **or**. two conditions, either of which is fatal:

| ground | cured by |
|---|---|
| an unpaid franchise tax balance | payment (step 3) |
| ⛔ **no completed annual report on file for the relevant period** | **a REPORT**, per year. money does not cure it |

⭐ **so a revived corporation that paid in full can still be refused a
certificate** — the second ground is independent, and it is the one a payment
mindset skips.

⚠️ **and note which years this reaches.** § 312(g) caps the *revival payment* at
the balance due on the **void date**, so the years **inside** the void window
can look settled. § 502(f) is not keyed to the void date at all — it asks
whether a **completed report** is on file for *"the relevant time period."*

🚩 **"the relevant time period" is undefined in chapter 5** and I have no source
for how the Division applies it. **ask.** the answer decides whether every
void-window year needs its own report before a certificate will issue.

⛔ **do not infer the answer from the § 312(g) cap.** that cap is about what you
**pay**; § 502(f) is about what you **file**. § 510 makes the same split — it
voids a charter for an unfiled complete annual report **independently** of the
tax.

### 🚨 SECOND — a THIRD bar lives in chapter 1, and § 502(f) gives no hint of it

> added 2026-09-14. § 502(f) reads as a complete list of the grounds that block
> a certificate. it is not. the third is **§ 103(c)(4)**, and it sits in a
> different chapter of the DGCL — which is precisely why a reader who checks
> § 502 concludes the question is settled.

> "The Secretary of State **shall not issue a certificate of good standing** with
> respect to any corporation with an **instrument held in suspension** pursuant
> to this subsection."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 103(c)(4),
> https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc01/index.html

⭐ **this produces the symptom most likely to be misread in the whole route.** you
file the renewal, you order the certificate to confirm it landed, and the office
declines. the natural read is *"the revival was refused."* the statutory read may
be the opposite — **the instrument is alive, held in suspension, and a cure
window is open.**

| what you observe | ground | what it means | what cures it |
|---|---|---|---|
| certificate refused, balance shows unpaid | § 502(f) | tax still owed | pay (step 3) |
| certificate refused, balance shows clear | § 502(f) | a **report** is absent for the relevant period | file the report |
| certificate refused, and a rejection notice went to the filer | ⛔ **§ 103(c)(4)** | an instrument is **in suspension** | a replacement instrument **AND a re-tender of the taxes and fees**, **within 5 business days of their notice** |
| entity itself still reads void | none of the above | the instrument was rejected **outright**, without suspension | re-file from scratch; the original date is lost |

⛔ **the three grounds are independent and can be true at once.** a clean tax
balance rules out the first and says naught about the other two.

### 🚨 so the FIRST question on a refusal is not about money

ask it in this order, because only the third carries a live clock:

1. **is an instrument of ours held in suspension?** — § 103(c)(4). if yes, **a
   5-business-day cure window is open or has already expired**, and it started
   when notice reached *the filer*, who may be an agent rather than you.

   ⛔ **and the cure is TWO acts on ONE clock.** the statute conditions the
   saved date on:

   > "...upon delivery of a replacement instrument in proper form for filing
   > **and tender of the required taxes and fees** **within 5 business days
   > after notice of such suspension is given to the filer**, the Secretary of
   > State shall establish as the filing date of such instrument the date and
   > time that would have been the filing date of the rejected instrument had it
   > been accepted for filing."
   >
   > — 8 Del. C. § 103(c)(4)

   a corrected paper delivered without the money re-tendered does not cure — it
   is a fresh non-filing under § 103(c)(2), on the same reasoning that makes the
   original filing a two-part act. **ask what the office still holds of your
   original tender, and what must be re-sent.**

   ⚠️ and read the clock's start: *"after notice ... is given to the filer."*
   not after you learned of it. if the notice went to an agent, days are already
   spent.
2. is there an unpaid franchise tax balance? — § 502(f), first ground.
3. is a completed annual report on file for *"the relevant time period"*? —
   § 502(f), second ground.

⚠️ **question 1 is time-critical and the other two are not.** an unpaid balance
and an absent report wait for you indefinitely; a suspension does not. so the
order above is not a preference — it is the only order in which a refusal can be
triaged without loss.

🎯 **and ask question 1 even when a rejection notice never reached you.** the
statute starts the clock at notice **to the filer**, and a refused certificate
may be the first signal you get that a notice went somewhere else.

⇒ the full read of § 103(c)(4) — what the 5-day cure buys, and why the office's
power to suspend is discretionary rather than owed — is in
`4.file.revival.stone`.

### ⚠️ Delaware sells TWO good-standing certificates, and they are 3.5× apart

ask the requester which one they want **before** you order.

| item | cost | same-day | 24-hour |
|------|------|----------|---------|
| Good Standing — **short form** | **$50.00** | +$50.00 | +$40.00 |
| Good Standing — **long form** | **$175.00** | +$80.00 | +$60.00 |
| certified copy (each) | $50.00 + $2.00/page | +$60.00 | +$50.00 |
| special certificate (references void, name change, merger) | $50.00 | +$50.00 | +$40.00 |

> ```
> Good Standing Short form:
>   All Entities Domestic and Foreign     $50.00 (per certificate)    $50.00    $40.00
> Good Standing Long form:
>   All Entities Domestic                $175.00 (per certificate)    $80.00    $60.00
> ```
> — [DE Division of Corporations, Fee Schedule, revised 2026-08-01](https://corpfiles.delaware.gov/Fee_Schedule/AugustFee2026.pdf)

> ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-14 — the schedule is embedded**, and all four rows above
> were checked against it cell by cell. the source of record is
> `registrator/briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/effectiveAt=2026-08-01.de-doc-fee-schedule.{pdf,txt}`
> — 254,794 bytes, extract 34,625 chars, revision read off the **body**, which
> carries exactly one date token: `Revised August 1, 2026`.
>
> ⛔ **the table above sits on a DIFFERENT AXIS than the fee table in
> `4.file.revival.stone`, and that is the whole mechanism of the `+$100` defect
> retracted below.** the schedule runs two tables with different column counts:
>
> ```
> corporate     Certificate Type | State Filing Fee | Certified Copy | Same Day | 24-Hour
> certificates  Certificate Type | Fee              | Same Day       | 24-Hour
> ```
>
> the certificates table has **no certified-copy column at all**. so a reader who
> carries the corporate header across lands every figure one column to the left,
> and a same-day fee reads as a certified-copy fee. **every cell stays true and
> the row becomes false** — which is why no check of the individual numbers would
> ever have caught it. read the header that sits above the row you are in.
>
> ⚠️ **a stale fee schedule has already cost this matter once**, in the direction
> that punishes the careful reader: a pull that succeeded against an **old
> revision** retracted a correct figure and shipped the retraction as verified.
> the check that closes it is a read of the revision stamp off the **document** —
> the slug `AugustFee2026.pdf` agrees here, but that agreement is the *result* of
> the check, never a substitute for it.

⭐ **for a corporation just revived, the LONG form is usually what is wanted.**
the short form states only that the entity is in good standing today; the long
form lists the entity's full history of prior filings. a bank or an investor who
asks for proof after a void period asks about the **history**, which is exactly
what the short form omits.

⛔ **a special certificate is a third option, at `$50.00`** — it references a
specific event such as a void. where a counterparty wants confirmation the void
was cured rather than the whole history, this is the cheap precise answer.

> 🚨 **this table read `$50` flat with `expedited +$100`.**
>
> the `$50` was the **short form** only, unlabeled — so it read as *the* price
> of a certificate of status and hid the $175 option entirely.
>
> ⚠️ **and `+$100` matches NEITHER form.** short form expedite is `$50.00`
> same-day / `$40.00` 24-hour; long form is `$80.00` / `$60.00`. the figure was
> carried over from the `$200.00` / `$100.00` column pair that applies to
> *filings* — but certificates are priced on a different table entirely, with
> its own columns.
>
> ⭐ **this is the third expedite-column defect found in this route on one day**
> — the others in `4.file.revival.stone` and
> `corp.shares.reduce/templates/3.file.amendment.stone`. in every one the
> headline fee was right and the expedite figure was borrowed from a row it
> does not belong to.

order at: https://corp.delaware.gov/

