## ⭐ then restore your OWN record — the §211(c) duty Delaware never asks about

good standing with the State is half of restoration. the other half is a duty
under the DGCL that no agency will bill you for and no status page will show.

> "(c) A failure to hold the annual meeting at the designated time or to elect
> a sufficient number of directors to conduct the business of the corporation
> **shall not affect otherwise valid corporate acts or work a forfeiture or
> dissolution of the corporation** except as may be otherwise specifically
> provided in this chapter. If the annual meeting for election of directors is
> not held on the date designated therefor **or action by written consent to
> elect directors in lieu of an annual meeting has not been taken, the directors
> shall cause the meeting to be held as soon as is convenient.** If there be a
> failure to hold the annual meeting or to take action by written consent to
> elect directors in lieu of an annual meeting **for a period of 30 days after
> the date designated for the annual meeting**, or if no date has been
> designated, **for a period of 13 months** after the latest to occur of the
> organization of the corporation, its last annual meeting or the last action by
> written consent to elect directors in lieu of an annual meeting, **the Court of
> Chancery may summarily order a meeting to be held upon the application of any
> stockholder or director.** The shares of stock represented at such meeting,
> either in person or by proxy, and entitled to vote thereat, **shall constitute
> a quorum for the purpose of such meeting, notwithstanding any provision of the
> certificate of incorporation or bylaws to the contrary.** The Court of Chancery
> may issue such orders as may be appropriate, including, without limitation,
> orders designating the time and place of such meeting, the record date or dates
> for determination of stockholders entitled to notice of the meeting and to vote
> thereat, and the form of notice of such meeting."
>
> — [8 Del. C. § 211(c)](https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc07/index.html),
> verified against
> `registrator/briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/de-title8-ch1-sc07-meetings.txt`

> ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-14 — subchapter VII is pulled and embedded.** 76,886 chars,
> url earned from the chapter-1 index's own nav rather than pattern-matched, and
> the body confirmed to carry `Subchapter VII. Meetings, Elections, Voting and
> Notice` before any quote was taken from it. this closes the whole cluster: the
> window arithmetic below, and every § 212 / § 228 / § 232 claim elsewhere in
> this route, all sit in this one container.
>
> 🚨 **and the pull found a defect the flag did not predict — THE QUOTE WAS
> TRUNCATED, by more than half.** the old version ended at *"as soon as is
> convenient."* three further sentences follow, and the first of them is the
> operative remedy:
>
> | what the old quote showed | what § 211(c) actually carries |
> |---|---|
> | a savings clause + a soft duty | ⛔ **plus a 30-day / 13-month deadline** |
> | no consequence named | ⛔ **plus a Court of Chancery summary order, on the application of ANY stockholder or director** |
> | — | ⛔ **plus a quorum override that defeats your own bylaws** |
>
> ⭐ **the truncation cut at a sentence boundary, so it read as a complete
> subsection** — the eye supplies a period the legislature did not write. that is
> the same mechanism as the § 224 proviso dropped from `0.gather.position.stone`.
>
> ⚠️ **the window arithmetic below was already correct** — it states the 30-day
> and 13-month limits and the summary-order remedy. but it sits ~30 lines *after*
> the block quote, and **the block quote is where a reader meets § 211(c) first.**
> a correction that never travels back to the head of its own section is a
> correction only the second reader gets.

read the two halves, because they point opposite ways:

| clause | what it does for you |
|---|---|
| *"shall not ... work a forfeiture or dissolution"* | ⭐ **a savings clause.** the annual meetings you missed while void did **not** compound the void. the void came from the franchise tax statute, and only from there |
| *"the directors **shall** cause the meeting to be held as soon as is convenient"* | ⛔ **a live, mandatory duty** — and it survives the revival. the savings clause forgives the past; it does not cancel the obligation |

⚠️ § 312(i) makes the **void period count** inside § 211(c)'s 30-day and
13-month windows, so a corporation revived after a multi-year void is already
far past them. past the window, *"the Court of Chancery may summarily order a
meeting to be held upon the application of any stockholder or director."*

⭐ **for a sole-founder corporation that risk is inert — but for a reason that
matters.** the windows apply in full; what makes them harmless is the
**applicant set**. only a stockholder or a director may apply, and there is one
of each, and he is the same person. add a second stockholder and the analysis
changes that day.

### the cheap instrument

§ 211(c) names its own substitute, four separate times: **"action by written
consent to elect directors in lieu of an annual meeting."** that is a full
substitute, not a lesser form.

| you have | what discharges the duty |
|---|---|
| a sole stockholder | **one signed written consent.** no meeting, no notice, no quorum |
| several stockholders | a written consent per your bylaws, or hold the meeting |

| item | value |
|---|---|
| fee | **none** |
| filed with Delaware | ⛔ **no** — this is an internal record |
| where it lives | the minute book |
| when | after the revival lands |

#### ⭐ and § 312(i) names a SECOND free discharge — one you may have already used

the same subsection that counts the void period against you
closes with a sentence that can settle the duty outright:

> "A **special meeting of stockholders held in accordance with subsection (h) of
> this section shall be deemed an annual meeting of stockholders for purposes of
> § 211(c)** of this title."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 312(i), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc12/index.html

⭐ **if you had no directors and held the § 312(h) special meeting to elect a
board, that meeting IS your annual meeting.** the duty is discharged by the act
you already performed to make the revival possible — no consent, no second
meeting, no fee.

| your position at revival | what discharges § 211(c) |
|---|---|
| directors were in office (the deemed board authorized it) | the **written consent** above |
| ⛔ **no directors — stockholders elected a board under (h)** | ✅ **that meeting already did it**, per § 312(i) |

⚠️ **read the condition: "in accordance with subsection (h)."** the rule applies
to the (h) meeting — the one called *"by any officer or stockholder upon notice
given in accordance with § 222"* to elect directors. an informal conversation
among the same people is not that meeting. **minute it as an (h) meeting when
you hold it**, or you forfeit a discharge you had earned.

> § 312(h) deems the board to be whoever *would* be directors but for the void,
> so a signer exists either way — but a post-revival date is the clean record.

⛔ **do not skip this because it is free and unobserved.** skip it and you hold
a corporation that is current on tax, restored on the register, and carries no
record that a board was ever elected. that is the gap a diligence request finds,
and it surfaces at the worst moment — mid-raise, mid-sale, mid-loan.

